%N A400400 Decimal expansion of the solution to I_0(t*sqrt(2)) = I_1(t*sqrt(2))*sqrt(2), where I_k is the order-k modified Bessel function of the first kind.
%C A400400 After an idea of Axel von Brömssen: Consider a football match where the two teams score according to independent Poisson processes with intensity 1 (expected number of goals per time unit) for the favorite team and 1/2 for the underdog team. Sequence gives the decimal expansion of the match length that maximizes the probability that the underdog wins. A400401 gives the maximum probability.
%C A400400 In general, if the score intensity of the underdog is s < 1, the optimal match length t for an underdog win satisfies I_0(t*2*sqrt(s)) = I_1(t*2*sqrt(s))/sqrt(s).
%C A400400 See A400399 for a discrete version.
%C A400400 Equals the value of t that maximizes P(S >= 1) where S has a Skellam(t/2,t)-distribution.
%H A400400 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A400400/a400400.png">Plot of the outcome probabilities as functions of match length</a>.
%H A400400 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessel_function">Bessel function</a>.
%H A400400 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skellam_distribution">Skellam distribution</a>.
%e A400400 1.45537806358935197705919750710477505690422167776044...
%o A400400 (Python)
%o A400400 from mpmath import mp,besseli,findroot
%o A400400 mp.dps = 110
%o A400400 t0 = findroot(lambda t:besseli(0,t*mp.sqrt(2)) - mp.sqrt(2)*besseli(1,t*mp.sqrt(2)),2)
%o A400400 print([int(d) for d in str(t0)[:101] if d != '.'])
%Y A400400 Cf. A400399, A400401.
%K A400400 nonn,cons,new
%O A400400 1,2
%A A400400 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 07 2026
